    "result_text": "There is only one test class in the repo, and it passes, as does the full suite. \n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the soft-duplicate (\"possible duplicate\") rule for owner creation, keeping structural impact low by adding cohesive units rather than growing the existing complex methods.\n\n**Behavior:** When a new owner is not a hard duplicate but shares an existing owner's `lastName` and `postcode` with a *different* telephone, it is still created and flagged with `possibleDuplicate: true` and `possibleDuplicateOf` set to the matching owner's id; otherwise `possibleDuplicate: false`.\n\n**Changes (5 files):**\n1. **`PossibleDuplicate.java`** (new) \u2014 a tiny, self-contained detection helper (`WMC_other \u2248 0`), mirroring the existing `CustomerCode`/`HouseholdId` pattern. It holds all the matching logic (last name + postcode match, different telephone).\n2. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 only 3 added lines that call the helper and set the fields, keeping the large method's cyclomatic complexity essentially unchanged (the expensive lever, since the controller class has high `WMC_other`).\n3. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 two persisted fields plus trivial (CC=1) accessors, so the flag survives to the `GET /api/owners/{id}` read the test uses.\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 two read-only DTO properties (`possibleDuplicate`, `possibleDuplicateOf`), auto-mapped by MapStruct via matching names.\n5. **`db/hsqldb/schema.sql`** \u2014 two columns (the test profile uses `ddl-auto=none`).\n\nThe dominant term (edits inside the complex `addOwner`) is held to 3 simple lines with no CC increase; all real logic lives in the new low-complexity class.",
